--TEST--
`unset()` takes an array element of a named holder and nothing else — a bare local and a static property are `E0234`/`E0413`, `rule:classes/unset-is-refused-on-a-property` and `rule:types/declaration`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Holder {
    public static array<string> $rows = [];

    public static function rows(): array<string> {
        return ["a" => "x"];
    }
}

int $x = 1;
// `rule:types/declaration`: a binding is declared once, with a type, and is definitely
// assigned. There is no "undefined again" state to put `$x` back into, so
// PHP's commonest `unset()` spelling has nothing to mean here.
unset($x);
// `rule:types/arrays` separates the array before the entry is removed, and the
// separated copy needs a slot to be written back into. A call's result has
// none, which is the same thing PHP means by "temporary expression in write
// context".
unset(Holder::rows()["a"]);
// `rule:classes/unset-is-refused-on-a-property`'s property refusal reads the same on a static: it is one
// slot, and `rule:classes/definite-property-initialization` guarantees it is definitely initialized for good.
unset(Holder::$rows);
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0234]: `unset()` takes an array element of a named holder
%A
error[E0234]: `unset()` takes an array element of a named holder
%A
error[E0413]: `unset()` on `Holder::$rows` is refused; a declared property can never become uninitialized again
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
